crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Chaitanya", "family": "Malaviya", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Ben", "family": "Bogin", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Ofir", "family": "Press", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Jonathan", "family": "Berant", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "1643", "event": {"name": "Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing", "location": "Miami, Florida, USA", "start": {"date-parts": [[2024, 11]]}, "end": {"date-parts": [[2024, 11]]}}, "container-title": ["Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2024, 11, 27]], "date-time": "2024-11-27T22:32:13Z", "timestamp": 1732746733000}, "score": 26.718014, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.505"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2024]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.505", "published": {"date-parts": [[2024]]}}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 11, 17]], "date-time": "2025-11-17T11:38:11Z", "timestamp": 1763379491972, "version": "3.45.0"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2020, 7, 16]]}, "group-title": "PsyArXiv", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Center for Open Science", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2020, 7, 16]], "date-time": "2020-07-16T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1594857600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<p>Demographic perception\u2014the perception of social quantities of geopolitical scale and social significance\u2014has been extensivelystudied in cognitive and political science (Citrin &amp;amp; Sides, 2008; Gilens, 2001; Herda, 2013). Regular patterns of over- and under-estimation emerge. Americans greatly overestimate, for instance, the proportion of citizens that identify as gay or Muslim, while underestimating those that are Christian. While these errors have been attributed to social factors such as fear of specific minorities (Gallagher, 2003; Wong, 2007), other work has suggested that these patterns result from the psychophysics of the perception of proportions (Landy, Guay &amp;amp; Marghetis 2018). A Bayesian formulation suggests that biases in the estimation of both social proportions and simple visual properties result from a common source: \u2018hedging\u2019 uncertain information toward a prior. Here we present a novel lab paradigm and two experiments that manipulate uncertainty in a simple (dot estimation) task, verifying the core assumptions of the Bayesian approach.</p>", "DOI": "10.31234/osf.io/zu4jx", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2020, 7, 16]], "date-time": "2020-07-16T11:35:30Z", "timestamp": 1594899330000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 1, "title": ["Scaling Uncertainty in Visual Perception and Estimation Tasks"], "prefix": "10.31234", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2167-8585", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Eleanor Brower", "family": "Schille-Hudson", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"given": "David", "family": "Landy", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "15934", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 11, 17]], "date-time": "2025-11-17T11:34:46Z", "timestamp": 1763379286000}, "score": 26.53463, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://osf.io/zu4jx_v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2020, 7, 16]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/zu4jx", "published": {"date-parts": [[2020, 7, 16]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 27]], "date-time": "2025-09-27T15:40:12Z", "timestamp": 1758987612258, "version": "3.44.0"}, "publisher-location": "400 Commonwealth Drive, Warrendale, PA, United States", "standards-body": {"name": "SAE International", "acronym": "SAE"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "SAE International", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "DOI": "10.4271/air771", "type": "standard", "created": {"date-parts": [[2017, 3, 8]], "date-time": "2017-03-08T10:37:36Z", "timestamp": 1488969456000}, "approved": {"date-parts": [[1992, 6, 1]]}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["1992 Visual Simulation for General Flight Simulator/Trainer Attachment"], "prefix": "10.4271", "author": [{"name": "G-10G Realistic Training Committee", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "2796", "link": [{"URL": "https://saemobilus.sae.org/downloads/standards/air771/Full%20Text%20PDF", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 27]], "date-time": "2025-09-27T15:01:54Z", "timestamp": 1758985314000}, "score": 26.06638, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://saemobilus.sae.org/standards/air771-1992-visual-simulation-general-flight-simulator-trainer-attachment"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[null]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.4271/air771"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 2, 21]], "date-time": "2025-02-21T01:13:46Z", "timestamp": 1740100426851, "version": "3.37.3"}, "reference-count": 13, "publisher": "Keldysh Institute of Applied Mathematics", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2021]]}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Currently, computer graphics programs (CG) as a tool for designing lighting installations allows us to move to a fundamentally new approach to assessing the quality of lighting: to move away from designing based on illuminance values to designing based on synthetic images or lighting design. Modern CG programs allow us to calculate the spatial-angular distribution of luminance (SADL). Still, some criterion is needed for the assessment, which will allow us to uniquely determine the quality of lighting in terms of luminance distribution. In this paper, we consider constructing a physiological model of the visual sense scale for assessing the quality of lighting according to SADL based on an experiment to assess visual sense from the luminance of a light source in the field of view on a uniform background with different luminance. As a result of the work done, it is proposed to use a scale of sensations in the form of a certain space. The axis of space is the threshold contrast for the resulting sense of discomfort, depending on the observation conditions. All other sensations are separated from the axis at some distance. Threshold contrasts, at which visual sensations arise within the framework of the proposed scale, can be used in the future to assess the storm in the lighting scene using a new criterion and will allow analysing the quality of lighting using CG methods.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.20948/graphicon-2021-3027-818-825", "type": "proceedings-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2021, 12, 14]], "date-time": "2021-12-14T10:38:05Z", "timestamp": 1639478285000}, "page": "818-825", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Scaling of Discomfort Sensations in the Conditions of Performing Office Visual Tasks"], "prefix": "10.20948", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9725-4266", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "George", "family": "Boos", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "National Research University \"Moscow Power Engineering Institute\""}], "role": [{"role